# `arvo-numeral`: what is proved, and how

Canon this design declares:
`ruling::the_format_spine_is_canon`, for the oracle every operation of the value is held against, the
format's own.
`ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path`, for the refusals held as compile failures and the
lowered path held by reading it.

This page is the proof of the value type the crate's design describes: which suites hold which
operations, over which shapes, against which oracle, and which mutants show each suite seeing what it
claims to see. The design states what the value is and does; what follows says how much of that is
established by a run and where the runs stop.

The suite is split into one test binary per width band, 1 to 8, 9 to 16, 17 to 32 and 33 to 64, so a
band's run is its own and the narrow bands stay quick.

Widths 1 to 8 are exhaustive: every signature over both slot families, both overflow policies under the
`Quiet` check and all five modes, every pair of members, through the value type and checked
against the oracle. The same widths are computed in each of the four rungs, which is the derived carrier
checked against every wider one, the 64-bit carrier among them. Widths 9 to 12 are computed in every
rung wide enough to carry them, the 16, 32 and 64-bit ones, both slot families, both policies, every
pair, against the oracle.

So the rungs are exhaustive at widths 1 to 12, which covers the 8-bit carrier at its full width and the
wider carriers below theirs, and the value type is exhaustive at 1 to 8. Widths 13 to 64 are sampled,
and every full-width branch of the 16, 32 and 64-bit carriers, the machine's own wrapping or saturating
add, falls among them: at width 16 alone there are four billion pairs per family and policy.
One fixed-point signature at a non-zero exponent, `UFixed<6, 3>`, is exhaustive the same way through the
value type: unsigned, as `UFixed` is, both policies, all five modes, every pair, against the oracle.
The exponent moves no slot of a sum, so this is the test that the sum reads the slot and never the
position. Its width sits below its 8-bit carrier's, so the rung's adaptation acts on every sum that
leaves the range and the 8-bit mutant turns it red; at the carrier's full width the adaptation is the
machine's own and no mutant of it could.

The difference and the product are proved at every place the sum is, on the same pairs, the
difference against the format's `sub` and the product against the tests' own arithmetic described
below, and the negation on every member against `neg`: through the value type at
widths 1 to 8, in every rung at widths 1 to 8 and in the three wide rungs at 9 to 12, on the edge
lattice and the seeded pairs above that. The rung product is asked only at exponent zero, which is the
only place it is called. The unsigned 64-bit integer is left out of the product's pairs, since the
value's product is refused there, and is the compile-fail case below.

The value computes the sum, the difference, the negation and the product at exponent zero in its
carrier, and the format computes them on wide slots, so for those the format's function is a second
computation and agreeing with it is evidence. The product at another exponent, the quotient and the
conversion are handed to the format's function, and comparing the value with the function it calls
would show nothing. Those three are proved against arithmetic the tests carry themselves: a quotient
of two wide integers rounded by a mode, and a completion onto a range by wrapping or by saturation,
written in the test crate and calling nothing of the format to compute. The mode, the policy, the
range and the exponent are read from the signature's declarations.

The product is where the exponent does move the slot, so the fixed-point product has a test of its
own: `Fixed<6, -3>` and `UFixed<6, -3>`, every pair, all five modes and both policies, against the
tests' own arithmetic, the slots' product scaled back onto the grid, and the same at the positive
exponent of `UFixed<6, 3>`. A control computes the slots' product with no scaling and has to disagree
with the value somewhere at each of the three formats. One pair is named in each mode, the
members at slots `-3` and `4` of the signed format, whose product sits at minus one and a half slots:
a tie below zero, which goes to `-1` toward zero, on the ceiling and under `half_up`, and to `-2` on
the floor and under `half_even`. `half_up` is the tie toward positive infinity at every sign, and not
the `HALF_UP` of Java or Python: a tie at -2.5 goes to -2.

Widths 9 to 64 run through the value type on an edge lattice, each end of the range and its neighbours,
zero and its neighbours, and every single-bit value, against the oracle, plus pairs drawn from a seeded
generator whose seed is committed in the test.

`fits_add`, `fits_sub` and `fits_neg` are checked against `panic_on_overflow` of the
format's own positions on the same pairs. `fits_mul` is that expression in the source, so it is
checked against whether the tests' own rounded product is in the range. `slot` returns the member a value was built from, and carrier
equality is slot equality.

The debug check is observed where it acts, `div`, `convert` and `from_index` among the sites. One test per site, `add` and `adapted`, and per policy,
`Wrap` and `Saturate`, gives the site an input that leaves the range under `Abort`, left to the default
under `Wrap` and named under `Saturate`, and expects the site's own message; the abort is that check's
specified behaviour in a debug build, so these are `#[should_panic]` tests. Each has a twin under `Quiet` taking
the same input and completing it by the policy, one test shows `Abort` stays quiet on what fits, and
one that the two checks hold the same member in the same size. The binary is compiled only under
`debug_assertions`, since a release build has no abort to observe. Its release twin, compiled only without
`debug_assertions` and run by `cargo test --release`, takes the same inputs under `Abort` and shows them
completed by the policy, since the check is a constant branch that clears there.
The door's check asks a verdict on the host integer that is not public, so it is observed at every
width from 1 to 64, both ranges, both policies and the five modes: `from_index` under `Abort` aborts on
exactly the host integers that name no member, for the 8-bit and the 64-bit host integers of each sign,
at every value within 16 of zero, both ends of the host integer and both ends of the range with their
neighbours.

A mutant is a configuration flag of the compiler named `arvo_mutant_<name>`, and a mutant run sets one
with `RUSTFLAGS="--cfg arvo_mutant_<name>"`. It is a flag and not a cargo feature for the reason the
debug check is a parameter and not a feature: a feature is unified across a build, so a crate anywhere in
a consumer's build that turned a mutant on would change the arithmetic of every other crate under
signatures it never touched. A flag is set only by whoever runs the build at the top, and no manifest
can set it for a dependency. The manifest declares every flag to the compiler's configuration check, so
a misspelt one is reported rather than building the crate unmutated.

One mutant per rung, `arvo_mutant_8`, `arvo_mutant_16`, `arvo_mutant_32` and `arvo_mutant_64`,
removes the rung's adaptation. The tests assert agreement with the oracle under every build and expect
nothing of a mutant, so each mutant turns red the tests that run through its rung and leaves the others
green. The red output of each mutant's run is committed.

One mutant per operation beside those, each the place that operation goes wrong first. `arvo_mutant_sub`
pins the unsigned saturating difference below zero to the top of the range where the format gives
zero. `arvo_mutant_neg` leaves the lowest signed member where it is under saturation at the carrier's
width, where the format gives the highest. `arvo_mutant_mul` pins a saturating product that leaves the
machine integer to the top whatever its sign. Each turns its own operation's tests red and leaves the
other operations green, and each red run is committed.

The crossing is exhaustive through the value type at widths 1 to 8 over both slot families: for every
ordered pair of the two policies and every ordered pair of the five modes, every member crosses to the
same slot, equals the value the three public doors build under the target, and crosses back to the
value it left. One case is named, the top of an unsigned 8-bit range and one, whose sum is zero under
the wrapping signature and the top under the saturating one it crosses into. `arvo_mutant_under` sends the
top of the range to the bottom in a crossing, turns these red and leaves every operation's tests green,
and its red run is committed.

The quotient is proved through the value type against the tests' own arithmetic, the dividend's slot
over the divisor's scaled onto the grid: at widths 1 to 4 for every member divisor but zero, in both
slot families, over both policies, the five modes and every dividend. A few quotients over
`Fixed<6, -3>` are named with their slots written out, where the position falls between slots and the
mode decides, so the suite does not rest on the tests' arithmetic alone. `fits_div` is checked against
whether the rounded quotient is in the range, on the same pairs.

The three delegated operations are then proved at every shape a value is held for. A shape is a width
from 1 to 64, a signed or an unsigned range, the wrapping or the saturating policy, one of the five
modes, a radix and an exponent. The radixes are two, on the shipped fixed points, and ten and three,
on formats the tests declare the way a crate outside the format's would, three being the radix that
shares no factor with the carrier's. The exponents are a family of five per width and radix whose two
ends are the last exponents the format admits for that operation there, the highest and the deepest,
and a test per operation holds both ends against the format's own verdict at every width, both ranges
and the three radixes, admitted at each and refused one past it. The product's highest is the highest
exponent at which every product of two members is a position a slot index holds, and the quotient's the
highest at which the remainder's denominator fits at both ends of the range; the tests compute each in
their own arithmetic and do not read it from the format. Each width band of
sixteen at each radix is one test and asserts the 1600 signatures it ran, so a width or a shape that
dropped out of a macro would fail the count. The integer point, a format of its own beside the fixed
point at exponent zero, is taken at every width for the quotient as well.

The members a signature is run at are every member where the range has 256 or fewer, and above that
the edge lattice with 192 members drawn from the seeded generator. A dividend meets ten constant
divisors over a signed range and six over an unsigned one: the ends of the range, one, two and three
with their negations, an odd slot a third of the way up, and the slot of the number one. A product is
taken on every pair up to six bits and above that on the lattice against the ends and the neighbours of
zero, with seeded pairs.

The tests' arithmetic forms a ratio in the host's 128-bit integer where its terms fit, and in the wide
integer of the format's own suites where they do not, so no case is skipped for want of room. The wide
integer is shared with the format's tests as a file and is not the format's library code. One test
holds the two ways against each other on ratios both can form, across the modes and both policies.

The conversion is a law over two shapes, and the pairs of all shapes run to millions a radix, past what
a build can instantiate. So it is proved on five sweeps, each crossing two coordinates whole, and each
says what it holds fixed. Between every pair of widths: a source of each width and range onto a target
of each width and range, 16384 pairs of formats a radix, under both policies of the target, once with
both sides at exponent zero, where no mode acts and the policy decides every member the target does
not hold, and once from exponent minus one onto zero under `HalfEven`, where the mode decides most
members. Each pair of a width matrix also holds the wrapping value against the saturating expectation,
a planted wrong answer, and requires it to disagree wherever a member leaves the target's range, so
the matrix is shown to see the policy on every pair that loses something. Between every pair of
exponents: at each width and each pair of ranges, each exponent of a
family of five on the source against each on the target, under the ten signatures of the target; the
family's last member is the deepest exponent a conversion from exponent one admits at that width, and a
test holds that end against the format's verdict. At the positive edge: at each width, radix and pair
of ranges, the source at the highest exponent at which both ends of its range land on positions a slot
index holds, onto a target at exponent zero, and a source at exponent zero onto the target at the
highest exponent at which the remainder's denominator fits, under the ten signatures of the target; a
test holds both edges against the format's verdict at every width, both ranges and the three radixes,
admitted at each and refused one past it. Under every source signature: at each width and on each
range, the ten signatures of the source onto the ten of the target across two steps of exponent. From
minus one onto zero the mode decides and no member leaves the range, so a result that read the source's
mode would disagree there. `arvo_mutant_convert_mode` rounds by the source's mode in place of the
target's, and turns that step red. From zero onto minus one every member past the target's range is
completed by the policy, below the lowest member and above the highest on a signed range and above the
highest on an unsigned one, so a result that read the source's policy would disagree there.
`arvo_mutant_convert_policy` completes by saturation wherever the source's policy is saturation and by
the target's otherwise; it turns this step red and passes every other conversion sweep. The red runs of
both are committed, the policy mutant's over every conversion sweep. Between every pair of radixes: at each width and pair of ranges, each ordered pair of two, ten and three, both sides at
exponent minus one, under the ten signatures of the target. What no sweep holds is a pair differing in
width and in exponent at once under the modes other than `HalfEven`, or in width and in radix at once.
Thirteen named pairs of 32 and 64 bits change the width and the exponent together under all ten
signatures, and narrow sources are taken whole between the integer and the fixed points. Each pair of a
sweep is run at the source's members as above and at the source slots that land on, half a slot from
and a slot from zero and each end of the target's range. `fits_convert` is checked against whether the
rounded member is in the target's range on the same members, and a conversion between two signatures
over one format equals `under`.

The host door is proved against `adapted` at every width from 1 to 64, both ranges, both policies and
the five modes, for each of the ten host integers: every value within 1024 of zero that the host
integer has, which is all of an 8-bit one, both ends of the host integer with their neighbours, and
both ends of the signature's range with theirs. `index` returns what `from_index` was given on the edge
lattice at every width, in every host integer the range fits, and the lists of host integers per width
are whole, a host integer left out being one the build refuses there.

`arvo_mutant_div` returns the dividend where the quotient leaves the range, which is what the machine's
wrapping quotient gives for the lowest signed member over minus one. `arvo_mutant_convert` reads the
source's slot index as the target's and so drops the two exponents. `arvo_mutant_index` makes the saturating door
send a negative host integer to the top of an unsigned range, which is what a comparison done after a
cast gives. Each turns its own tests red and leaves the others green, and each red run is committed.

The fixed-point aliases are checked to be the written types. One product is named through `Fix`: a
half times three eighths, at eighths, sits at one and a half slots, and is slot 2 under `HalfEven`
and slot 1 under `Floor`. A preset names an adaptation for a fixed-point format and the alias reads
it.

The count has two test targets off one shared module, one per path, each built only under its
feature. Both read the lengths of lists of zero to four cells and the indices of positions in the
platform-sized integer, a byte, a signed integer and a fixed-point value, through code generic over
the signature and the check, and a count past the range under wrapping and under saturation. The const
target reads the same lengths as constants, one of them as an array's length, and names no feature of
the toolchain, which is the evidence that a consumer needs no gate. Under `Abort` a count past the
range aborts in a debug build on the plain path; on the const path it is a compile-fail pair, a list
of four cells in two bits beside one of three, and a second pair refuses a length in a format without
a one beside a format with one. `arvo_mutant_count` stops a count at one and turns both targets red, and
its red run is committed.

`law::additive_associativity_under_wrapping` and `law::additive_associativity_under_saturation` are
reproduced through the value type, with the registry's own counts.

Compile-fail pairs under `tests/ui/`, each beside a passing twin that differs in the offending token,
with the diagnostic committed: width 0 and 65, `constant` of a non-member, the additive identity on a
format without one, `add` across two signatures, a host integer where a value goes and a value where a
host integer goes, the field named from outside, `Clamp`, `Stochastic` and a phased format. For this
round's operations: `sub` across two signatures, `mul` on the unsigned 64-bit integer beside the same
product at 63 bits, and the multiplicative identity on a one-bit signed integer beside a two-bit one.
For the override: a value under `WithOverflow` naming `Clamp` beside one naming `Saturate`, a value
under `WithRounding` naming `Stochastic` beside one naming `HalfEven`, and a crossing to a signature
over another format beside one to a sibling.
For the host door: `index::<u8>()` on a signed 8-bit integer beside `index::<i8>()`, and on a 9-bit
unsigned integer beside `index::<u16>()`.
The
admission is shown open from both sides: an outside slot family over part of its width's range is refused
beside one over the whole range, with its own row in the placement, which is held; an outside policy that
clamps is refused beside one that wraps, which is held; and an outside row in the placement that names
the 8-bit container for a 16-bit family and writes its own `SoleCarrier::ADMITTED` checking nothing is
refused at `Sole::ADMITTED`, beside the same disarmed row naming the 16-bit container, which is held. Beside them, the routes to the carrier's type:
`S::Repr` with `S: Sole`, a projection on `Sole` itself, and `Held` and `Machine` by their paths, each
beside a twin naming the slot instead.

A consumer is a crate of its own, outside this workspace, that depends on `arvo-numeral` with no feature
line and reaches every public item: each type, trait and alias, each associated item of `Sole` and
`DebugCheck`, each door and operation, the identity, the host doors of `USize` and `ISize`, and the
derived traits. It implements `DebugCheck` once and builds an alias through it. It declares a preset
of its own that implements `NamesAdaptation` for two formats and names a different adaptation for
each, and an alias of its own over `Uint` whose adaptation parameter defaults to that preset, which is
the whole of what shipping a default takes. It overrides one coordinate at a type, through
`WithOverflow`, and at a value, through `with_overflow`, `with_rounding` and `under`. A test builds and
runs it, and it asserts what it reads. The test takes the consumer's path and its scratch directory
when it runs, so a build directory shared between checkouts tests the checkout the run was started
in. The scratch directory is the one in the build directory the test binary sits under, found by the
tag file cargo writes at a build directory's root. A second test runs the test binary again with
another crate directory in its environment and reads back the path it resolved, which a path fixed at
build time would not follow. The rerun enters the same test, which prints the path and returns where
the variable the rerun sets is present, so no test in the binary passes without asserting. It names no feature, so it does not reach the count, whose two
targets are written as a consumer would write them.

The disassembly of `add` at 13 bits in 16 and at 64 bits beside the host's `wrapping_add` is committed as
an ad hoc reading, and called that: the bench harness cannot yet commit the disassembly of a named
function.
